# generic_sort.tkr — the §1.5 generic-stack capstone fixture for `sort<T: IOrd>`, the stable ascending
# merge sort whose order is dispatched through the element type's `IOrd` conformance (9-ops generic
# operator dispatch: `<=`/`<` lower to `operator __le`/`__lt` on `T`). A SELF-CONTAINED project (its
# own copy of the IEq/IOrd/IHash capability corpus + StrKey/I64Key conformers + a user `Ver` struct
# deriving IOrd, plus a private copy of the free generic `sort`), because the stdlib's teko::sort and
# teko::cmp are compiler-internal and not visible to a user project — the collections_fase1b precedent.
# The single scenario sorts three IOrd-conforming element types at three DISTINCT instantiations
# (`I64Key`, `StrKey`, `Ver`), asserts each emerges ascending (and that `Ver`'s equal-`major` run keeps
# its input `tag` order — STABILITY), and carries the `I64Key` order-sensitive checksum in the exit
# code: `[5,1,3,2,4]` sorts to `[1,2,3,4,5]` whose position-weighted sum is 55. A mis-order or a lost
# stability tie flips a verdict and moves the exit code off 55. Runs on the C backend.

Feature: generic_sort — stable generic sort<T: IOrd> over IOrd-conforming element types (§1.5)

  Scenario: sort_iord_conformers — I64Key/StrKey/Ver sort ascending and stably; the I64Key checksum is 55
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 55
